"Create a compact, publication-quality LaTeX/TikZ schematic on a pure white background, approximately 18 cm wide and 8 cm high, including the bottom annotation. Preserve the two-row, four-column arrangement, seven computational nodes, and all eight dependencies described below. Use generous whitespace, carefully aligned nodes, and no figure title.\n\nUse a restrained, flat vector aesthetic: rounded rectangular nodes with 2 mm corner radii, uniform 0.7 pt outlines, and subtle pastel fills. Avoid shadows, gradients, textures, decorative icons, and three-dimensional effects. Set descriptive labels in a clean sans-serif font at 9\u201310 pt, with modest semibold emphasis for node headings; use consistent LaTeX mathematical typography at 10\u201311 pt for equations. Keep text near-black (`#1F2937`) and preserve these sizes at final manuscript scale. Use comfortable internal padding and clear separation between headings and equations.\n\nApply the following coordinated palette:\n- Supplied input and target nodes: pale blue fill (`#EAF3FB`), muted blue outline (`#829DB4`).\n- Fixed baseline node: pale blue-gray fill (`#EEF2F6`), slate-gray outline (`#8795A5`).\n- Observed residual node: pale amber fill (`#FFF3DA`), muted ochre outline (`#B49B63`).\n- Mean residual and corrected prediction nodes: pale teal fill (`#E7F3EF`), muted teal outline (`#6B9D92`).\n- Evaluation node: very light lavender-gray fill (`#F1EFF6`), muted lavender-gray outline (`#9892A5`).\n\nArrange the computational elements as follows:\n\n1. **Upper left \u2014 supplied inputs.** A pale blue node headed \u201cInput observations,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{x}=(-2,-1,0,1,2).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lower left \u2014 supplied targets.** A matching pale blue node headed \u201cObserved targets,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{y}=2\\mathbf{x}+3\\mathbf{1}\n   \\]\n   and, on a second line,\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{y}=(-1,1,3,5,7).\n   \\]\n   Align the input and target nodes vertically and give them equal widths.\n\n3. **Upper second column \u2014 baseline prediction.** A pale blue-gray node headed \u201cFixed baseline,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{(0)}=2\\mathbf{x}\n   \\]\n   followed by\n   \\[\n   (-4,-2,0,2,4).\n   \\]\n   Present the expression and resulting vector as a straightforward arithmetic calculation.\n\n4. **Lower second column \u2014 observed residuals.** A pale amber node headed \u201cObserved residuals,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{y}-2\\mathbf{x}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{r}=(3,3,3,3,3).\n   \\]\n   Position it directly beneath the baseline node and horizontally beside the observed-target node. Match its width to the baseline node.\n\n5. **Lower third column \u2014 scalar correction.** A pale teal node headed \u201cMean residual,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   b=\\frac{1}{5}\\sum_{i=1}^{5}r_i=3.\n   \\]\n   Make this node slightly narrower than the vector-valued nodes, while retaining sufficient padding around the equation. Leave the upper third-column space open for the two prediction dependencies.\n\n6. **Upper right \u2014 corrected prediction.** A pale teal node headed \u201cCorrected prediction,\u201d containing\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\n   =2\\mathbf{x}+b\\mathbf{1}\n   \\]\n   and\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\n   =(-1,1,3,5,7).\n   \\]\n   Give the rightmost column sufficient width to accommodate these expressions without reducing the mathematical type size.\n\n7. **Lower right \u2014 evaluation.** A very light lavender-gray node headed \u201cEvaluate on the same five observations,\u201d wrapped across two balanced lines if needed. Beneath the heading, display\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{y}\\quad\\text{vs.}\\quad\n   \\widehat{\\mathbf{y}}^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}},\n   \\]\n   followed by\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\mathrm{MSE}\n   &=\\frac{1}{5}\\sum_{i=1}^{5}\n   \\left(y_i-\\widehat y_i^{\\,\\mathrm{corr}}\\right)^2\\\\\n   &=0.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Align this node directly beneath the corrected-prediction node. Use deliberate equation line breaks rather than compressed typography.\n\nDraw every dependency explicitly using solid arrows approximately 0.9 pt thick, with consistent, compact, filled LaTeX-style arrowheads. Use dark slate (`#334155`) for supplied-value and baseline dependencies. Use darker teal (`#227C70`) for the residual-to-mean and mean-to-correction dependencies. Keep arrow labels dark and legible, with a small white clearance around each label where needed.\n\n1. Draw a horizontal arrow from the input \\(\\mathbf{x}\\) node to the baseline node.\n2. Draw a downward arrow from the baseline node to the residual node.\n3. Draw a horizontal arrow from the observed-target node to the residual node.\n4. Draw a darker teal horizontal arrow from the residual node to the mean-residual node, labeled \u201caverage five residuals.\u201d Wrap this label compactly within the inter-node space if necessary, maintaining readable type and clear separation from both node outlines.\n5. Draw a long horizontal dark-slate arrow from the baseline node to the corrected-prediction node through the open upper third-column space. Place the label \\(2\\mathbf{x}\\) above the arrow.\n6. Draw a separate darker teal arrow from the mean-residual node to the corrected-prediction node. Route it upward from the mean node through the open third-column space, then rightward into a lower port on the corrected node\u2019s left edge. Keep this horizontal segment visibly below the baseline arrow and label it \\(b=3\\). Use a clean orthogonal elbow with a subtly rounded corner; the route must not intersect the baseline arrow.\n7. Draw a downward dark-slate arrow from the corrected-prediction node to the evaluation node.\n8. Draw a separate dark-slate target-data arrow from the observed-target node to the evaluation node. Route it downward into a dedicated channel beneath the lower row, horizontally below all intervening nodes, then upward into the evaluation node\u2019s bottom edge. Label the horizontal segment \u201cobserved targets: same five points.\u201d Reserve enough bottom margin for this route and label.\n\nKeep all arrowheads clearly outside node borders, maintain consistent label offsets, and avoid arrow crossings or lines passing through nodes. Balance column spacing around both node contents and connector labels; preserve the compact overall footprint without crowding the central scalar correction.\n\nBelow the bottom routing channel, place one small, centered, visually subordinate note in dark gray:\n\n\u201cThe correction is computed and evaluated on these same five observations.\u201d\n\nThe finished schematic should communicate the exact finite calculation and its data dependencies. It must not suggest held-out evaluation, predictive generalization, optimization, or additional experiments."